<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">United National Independence Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as UNIP</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United National Independence Party (UNIP) is a Zambian political party founded in 1959 and led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a>, successor to the banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a>. It led the campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> and won the elections of 1964 that brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> to independence as Zambia. It was the only legal party from 1972 to 1990 and lost power in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-national-independence-party.jpg" alt="United National Independence Party" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United National Independence Party · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Unip-logo-torch-white-bg.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>UNIP was founded in October 1959 by Mainza Chona as successor to the Zambian African National Congress, banned earlier that year, and Chona led it while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-kaunda">Kenneth Kaunda</a> was in prison; Kaunda took over after his release in 1960. In the general elections that followed UNIP came second with 14 seats behind the United Federal Party, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-nkumbula">Harry Nkumbula</a> of the Northern Rhodesian African National Congress, despite a secret pact with the UFP, chose to govern with UNIP. In 1964 UNIP won 55 of the 75 seats, and Kaunda, as prime minister, led the country to independence on 24 October 1964, when he became president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In 1968 Kaunda was re-elected with 82% of the vote and UNIP won 81 of the 105 elected seats. An amended constitution then made UNIP the sole legal party, in what was called a one-party participatory democracy. National policy was set by the party's Central Committee; UNIP's secretary general was the second person in the Zambian hierarchy, and its president, chosen at the party's general conference, was the sole candidate for president of the republic, confirmed every five years in a yes or no referendum. Voters chose among three UNIP candidates in each of the 125 constituencies, and Kaunda received at least 80% of the vote in 1978, 1983 and 1988.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Multi-party democracy returned at the end of 1990, and in the 1991 elections the Movement for Multi-Party Democracy won 125 seats to UNIP's 25, while Frederick Chiluba defeated Kaunda, who received 24% of the vote. After constitutional changes effectively barred Kaunda from running again, UNIP boycotted the 1996 elections. With Kaunda's son Tilyenji as candidate it won 10% of the presidential vote and 13 seats in 2001, joined the United Democratic Alliance in 2006, and from 2011 won no seat in the National Assembly, Tilyenji Kaunda receiving 0.24% of the vote in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United National Independence Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>United National Independence Party</b>, formed in October 1959, took Kaunda as leader on his release in January 1960.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaunda's <b>United National Independence Party</b> (UNIP) held power from 1964 to 1991, and from 1972 Zambia was a one-party state under the motto &quot;One Zambia, One Nation&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/state/zambia">Zambia</a></li></ul></section>
